# Multi-Stage Reasoning

## Overview

A customer's package is late and you want the model to weigh options, such as
reship, refund, or wait, before it answers. Tree of Thoughts, Graph of
Thoughts, and TRM split that work across two or three structured model calls.
Each stage has its own instruction and JSON schema, and Jido AI moves data
between stages in local code:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls}
# => {"Reship order A-1001 today with express shipping at no charge.", 3}
```

You'll run all three Flows offline, see the instruction for each stage, and
read the result data. Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Three Agents

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.TreeAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/tree_agent", vsn: 1, name: "tree_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.TreeOfThoughts
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.GraphAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/graph_agent", vsn: 1, name: "graph_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.GraphOfThoughts
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.RevisionAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/revision_agent", vsn: 1, name: "revision_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.TRM
  end
end
```

None of these Flows calls tools.

## The Stage Loop

Each Flow has a `ModelRound` that runs one stage and invokes itself for the
next one:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.{GraphOfThoughts, TreeOfThoughts, TRM}

for flow <- [TreeOfThoughts.ModelRound, GraphOfThoughts.ModelRound, TRM.ModelRound] do
  IO.puts("Graph: #{inspect(flow)}")

  flow.flow().components
  |> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 0))
  |> Enum.each(fn {name, component} -> IO.puts("  #{name}: #{component.kind}") end)
end
```

```text
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.TreeOfThoughts.ModelRound
  accept: call
  model: call
  next: invoke
  output_guard: call
  prepare: call
  project: call
  record_attempt: call
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.GraphOfThoughts.ModelRound
  advance: invoke
  model: call
  output_guard: call
  prepare: call
  project: call
  record_attempt: call
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.TRM.ModelRound
  advance: invoke
  model: call
  output_guard: call
  prepare: call
  project: call
  record_attempt: call
```

`prepare` sets the stage instruction and output schema. The instruction goes
after the conversation as a final system message, below the Agent
instructions. The `invoke` step reads the object, stores it in the stage
state, and starts the next `ModelRound` or completes. Only the last stage's
answer is added to the conversation; earlier stages appear in
`model_attempts` only.

## Offline Model

The fake provider answers by stage. It reads the last message of each request
(the stage instruction) and passes it to a function you supply:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LLM do
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 1536,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, __MODULE__}]
    }
  end

  def stub(notebook, answer) do
    Req.Test.stub(__MODULE__, fn conn ->
      {:ok, body, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
      [%{"text" => instruction} | _parts] = List.last(Jason.decode!(body)["input"])["content"]
      send(notebook, {:stage, instruction})
      object(conn, answer.(instruction))
    end)
  end

  defp object(conn, object) do
    Req.Test.json(conn, %{
      "id" => "resp_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "output" => [
        %{
          "type" => "message",
          "id" => "msg_1",
          "role" => "assistant",
          "status" => "completed",
          "content" => [
            %{"type" => "output_text", "text" => Jason.encode!(object), "annotations" => []}
          ]
        }
      ],
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 80,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 40,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 120
      }
    })
  end
end
```

`ask` runs one request on a fresh Agent and returns the reply with the stage
instructions in order:

```elixir
notebook = self()
question = "Order A-1001 is five days late. What should we offer the customer?"

ask = fn agent_module, answer ->
  MyApp.LLM.stub(notebook, answer)
  agent = agent_module.instantiate!(id: "agent-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
  signal = agent_module.request_signal!(%{input: question, options: MyApp.LLM.options()})
  {:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} = agent_module.cmd(agent, signal)

  stages =
    Stream.repeatedly(fn ->
      receive do
        {:stage, instruction} -> String.slice(instruction, 0, 48)
      after
        0 -> nil
      end
    end)
    |> Enum.take_while(& &1)

  {reply, stages}
end
```

## Tree of Thoughts

Tree of Thoughts generates candidates, scores them, keeps the top two, and
synthesizes an answer. Selection is local code, so that's three model calls:

| Stage | Instruction starts with | Required JSON |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Generate | `Generate four distinct candidate approaches` | `thoughts`: at least 2 `%{"content"}` |
| Evaluate | `Evaluate each candidate from this JSON list:` + candidates | `thoughts`: at least 2 `%{"content", "score"}`, score 0–1 |
| Select | No model call | Sort by score, keep 2 |
| Synthesize | `Synthesize the best final answer from this JSON list:` + the 2 kept | `answer` |

```elixir
candidates = [
  "Reship with express shipping",
  "Refund the shipping fee",
  "Wait two more days",
  "Offer a 10% coupon"
]

tree_answer = fn
  "Generate" <> _rest ->
    %{thoughts: Enum.map(candidates, &%{content: &1})}

  "Evaluate" <> _rest ->
    %{thoughts: Enum.zip_with(candidates, [0.9, 0.7, 0.2, 0.4], &%{content: &1, score: &2})}

  "Synthesize" <> _rest ->
    %{answer: "Reship order A-1001 today with express shipping at no charge."}
end

{reply, stages} = ask.(MyApp.TreeAgent, tree_answer)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls}
```

The result is
`{:completed, "Reship order A-1001 today with express shipping at no charge.", 3}`.

```elixir
stages
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  "Generate four distinct candidate approaches to t",
  "Evaluate each candidate from this JSON list: [{\"",
  "Synthesize the best final answer from this JSON "
]
```

```elixir
reply.data.reasoning
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  method: :tree_of_thoughts,
  thoughts: [
    %{content: "Reship with express shipping", score: 0.9},
    %{content: "Refund the shipping fee", score: 0.7},
    %{content: "Wait two more days", score: 0.2},
    %{content: "Offer a 10% coupon", score: 0.4}
  ],
  steps_count: 4,
  conclusion: "Reship order A-1001 today with express shipping at no charge."
}
```

`thoughts` holds every scored candidate in the model's order, not only the two
kept for synthesis. `steps_count` is the number of scored candidates.

## Graph of Thoughts

Graph of Thoughts asks for thought nodes and the edges between them, then
synthesizes from the graph. That's two model calls:

| Stage | Instruction starts with | Required JSON |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Construct | `Build a small graph of useful thoughts` | `nodes`: at least 1 `%{"id", "content"}`; `edges`: at least 1 `%{"from", "to", "relationship"}` |
| Synthesize | `Synthesize one final answer from this thought graph:` + the graph | `answer` |

Between the stages, Jido AI checks that every edge's `from` and `to` name an
existing node ID.

```elixir
graph_answer = fn
  "Build" <> _rest ->
    %{
      nodes: [
        %{id: "late", content: "Order is five days late"},
        %{id: "policy", content: "Late orders get free reshipping"},
        %{id: "offer", content: "Reship with express shipping"}
      ],
      edges: [
        %{from: "late", to: "policy", relationship: "triggers"},
        %{from: "policy", to: "offer", relationship: "supports"}
      ]
    }

  "Synthesize" <> _rest ->
    %{answer: "Our late-order policy covers this, so we'll reship express for free."}
end

{reply, stages} = ask.(MyApp.GraphAgent, graph_answer)

{reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls, length(stages)}
```

The result is
`{"Our late-order policy covers this, so we'll reship express for free.", 2, 2}`.

```elixir
Map.take(reply.data.reasoning, [:method, :edges, :steps_count])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  method: :graph_of_thoughts,
  edges: [
    %{from: "late", to: "policy", relationship: "triggers"},
    %{from: "policy", to: "offer", relationship: "supports"}
  ],
  steps_count: 3
}
```

`reasoning.nodes` holds the three nodes, and `steps_count` counts them.

An edge to a missing node stops the request after the first call:

```elixir
broken_graph = fn "Build" <> _rest ->
  %{
    nodes: [%{id: "late", content: "Order is five days late"}],
    edges: [%{from: "late", to: "refund", relationship: "suggests"}]
  }
end

{reply, _stages} = ask.(MyApp.GraphAgent, broken_graph)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :reason_code, :reason, :model_calls])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  status: :blocked,
  reason_code: :output,
  reason: "graph edges must reference existing node IDs",
  model_calls: 1
}
```

## TRM

TRM drafts an answer, critiques it, and revises it once. That's three model
calls:

| Stage | Instruction starts with | Required JSON |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Draft | `Create a strong initial draft answer` | `draft` |
| Critique | `Find the important errors or omissions in this draft:` + draft | `critique` |
| Revise | `Revise the draft with this critique and return the final answer:` + draft and critique | `answer` |

```elixir
revision_answer = fn
  "Create" <> _rest ->
    %{draft: "Sorry for the delay. Your order will arrive soon."}

  "Find" <> _rest ->
    %{critique: "The draft gives no date and no remedy for the delay."}

  "Revise" <> _rest ->
    %{answer: "Sorry for the delay. We reshipped A-1001 express; it arrives 2026-11-02."}
end

{reply, stages} = ask.(MyApp.RevisionAgent, revision_answer)

{reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls}
```

The result is
`{"Sorry for the delay. We reshipped A-1001 express; it arrives 2026-11-02.", 3}`.

```elixir
reply.data.reasoning
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  method: :trm,
  draft: "Sorry for the delay. Your order will arrive soon.",
  critique: "The draft gives no date and no remedy for the delay.",
  iterations: 1,
  steps_count: 3,
  conclusion: "Sorry for the delay. We reshipped A-1001 express; it arrives 2026-11-02."
}
```

`iterations` is always 1. There's no setting for more revision cycles.

## Limits and Parameters

None of these Flows has a breadth, depth, or iteration input. The stage count
is fixed in each Flow:

| Flow | Model calls | Retries | Default `options` | `reasoning` fields |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.TreeOfThoughts` | 3 | 0 | `max_tokens: 1536, temperature: 0.4` | `thoughts`, `steps_count`, `conclusion` |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.GraphOfThoughts` | 2 | 0 | `max_tokens: 1536, temperature: 0.3` | `nodes`, `edges`, `steps_count`, `conclusion` |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.TRM` | 3 | 0 | `max_tokens: 1536, temperature: 0.2` | `draft`, `critique`, `iterations`, `steps_count`, `conclusion` |

Every `reasoning` map also has `method`. The input schema is the same for all
three: `input`, `instructions`, `model`, `options`, `prompt_cache`, `context`
(`max_messages` default 64, `max_bytes` default 200,000), and `limits`.
`limits` takes `elapsed_ms` (default 120,000) and the optional
`input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `total_tokens`, and `total_cost` budgets.
They apply across all stages: a stage that would exceed one ends the request
as `:blocked` with `reason_code: :limits`. Unknown keys fail validation.

`instructions` replaces the Flow's default system message (read it with
`default_instructions/0`). The stage instructions and schemas are fixed.

## Stage Failures

Any stage can end the request:

| Cause | `status` | `reason_code` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Provider error, or text that isn't the stage's object | `:failed` | `:model` |
| Output guard rejected the stage | `:blocked` | `:output` |
| Graph edge names a missing node | `:blocked` | `:output` |
| A budget refused the next call | `:blocked` | `:limits` |

A failed request keeps what the finished stages produced: Tree of Thoughts
keeps scored `thoughts`, Graph of Thoughts keeps `nodes` and `edges`, and TRM
keeps `draft` and `critique`, with `steps_count` counting what's present and
`conclusion: nil`.

## Summary

- Tree of Thoughts makes 3 calls: generate, evaluate, synthesize. The top two
  scores are selected locally.
- Graph of Thoughts makes 2 calls: construct, synthesize. Edges are checked
  against node IDs before synthesis.
- TRM makes 3 calls: draft, critique, revise, with `iterations: 1`.
- Each stage sends its instruction as a final system message and a strict
  JSON schema. Only the final answer enters the conversation.
- Stage counts are fixed; `limits` budgets apply across all stages.

